programmers-screenshot (0.24.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * The update notice and the post-upgrade changelog are now windows rather
    than desktop notifications. A notification vanishes while you are looking
    somewhere else, and truncates a changelog to a couple of lines; a window
    waits to be dismissed and scrolls.
  * The whole changelog entry is shown, rather than the first three bullets
    that fitted in a notification.
  * The package description no longer claims there are no dialogs. There have
    been since the settings window, and there are more now.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 05 Aug 2026 13:10:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.23.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the eraser ground to a halt after a few seconds of dragging, and
    recovered only when you let go. Two things grew with the length of a
    stroke and multiplied: the whole swept path was repainted every frame,
    and every hole made so far was punched into it again. Only the part that
    moved is repainted now, and only holes that could change a pixel in it
    are punched. Cost per frame no longer grows with the stroke.
  * Holes are also spaced by about the eraser's radius rather than every 4
    pixels. They were stacked four deep for no visual gain, and holding the
    eraser still used to add another on every mouse event.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 05 Aug 2026 11:40:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.22.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Optional update check. Switch it on in the settings window and the tool
    asks github.com once a day whether a newer release exists, and shows a
    notification with a button to the release notes if so. Off by default:
    this is the only network call the program makes.
  * The check runs in a process of its own after a capture has finished, so
    nothing can put a network timeout between the keypress and the overlay.
    It never downloads or installs anything.
  * Silent whenever there is nothing to say: up to date, offline, rate
    limited, or a reply that does not parse.
  * The first run after an upgrade now reports what changed, read from the
    changelog already shipped in the package. No network. Nothing on a first
    install, on a downgrade, or twice for the same version.
  * Bookkeeping moved to its own state.json, so machine state is not mixed
    into the preferences you edit.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 05 Aug 2026 09:15:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.21.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Related tools now share a button. Pen and Highlighter sit together under
    Ink; Black Bar and Pixelate under Redact. Eleven buttons become nine,
    which on the floating palette is two rows instead of three.
  * The redaction tool is now called Black Bar, since Redact is the name of
    the pair it belongs to.
  * A group button shows whichever member you last picked and remembers it,
    so the common case stays a single click; the corner wedge opens the rest.
  * Fix: the test suite read the real preferences file, so whatever was last
    chosen in the settings window changed how the tests behaved. It now uses
    a config of its own.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 16:05:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.20.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * The toolbar can now be a floating palette you drag around instead of a
    bar across the top of every monitor. Switch in the settings window; it
    takes effect at once and remembers where you put it. Bar remains the
    default and is unchanged.
  * One palette rather than one per monitor: you put it where you want it,
    and copies on the other screens would be clutter you could not remove.
  * Sub-tool flyouts. A tool with variants wears a wedge in the corner of its
    button; clicking it opens the alternatives beside the button, on
    whichever side has room. The line tool's line, rectangle, circle and
    arrow have moved there from the settings row, and the button now shows
    which of them is selected.
  * The pixelate test no longer captures the live screen. It compared how
    many colours survive coarse against fine blocks, which was a meaningless
    comparison whenever the desktop happened to be a flat expanse, and made
    the suite fail at random.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 14:20:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.19.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * New Eraser tool: drag across the canvas to rub out the parts of any mark
    you sweep through -- half a pen stroke, a bite out of a rectangle, a hole
    in a pixelate block or a redaction bar. A plain click with no drag
    removes the whole mark instead.
  * The holes are kept as geometry rather than as a bitmap mask, so they are
    as sharp in the saved PNG as they are on screen, whatever the display
    scale.
  * The rubbing out happens under the pointer as you drag, not when you let
    go.
  * One drag undoes as a single step, however many marks it crossed.
  * Note that rubbing out part of a redaction bar uncovers what it was
    hiding.
  * Erasing a numbered step badge renumbers the ones after it, so the
    sequence stays 1..n and the next badge continues instead of repeating a
    number already in use.

 -- Shubshub  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 10:30:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.18.2) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix: on a HiDPI display the overlay showed a hugely magnified crop of the
    top-left corner instead of the desktop. The capture arrives in physical
    pixels and the overlay draws in logical ones, and the on-screen paint did
    not account for the difference. The undimmed selection region had the
    same fault. Saved PNGs were never affected -- they were always rendered
    with the correct arithmetic -- but the overlay was unusable, since you
    could not see what you were selecting.

 -- Shubshub  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 20:15:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.18.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix: a setting changed in the settings window did not affect the capture
    you were holding, only the next one. Preferences were read once at
    startup, before the overlay opened, so switching saving off still wrote
    the screenshot to disk that first time. They are now read after the
    overlay closes, which is when the window has finished writing them.

 -- Shubshub  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.18.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * A settings button on the toolbar, opening a window for preferences that
    outlive a session. The first is whether captures are saved to a folder,
    and which folder. Stored in
    ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/preferences.json.
  * What the window stores is a default. An explicit --directory, --no-save
    or -o on the command line still wins.
  * --version now says where it loaded from, as well as the number. One
    version can cover several builds, since a fix between releases does not
    bump it, so the number alone cannot tell you which code is running.
  * build.sh --install now passes --reinstall. Without it apt saw the same
    version already installed and did nothing, leaving you running the
    previous build.

 -- Shubshub  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 18:05:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.17.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Saved screenshots are now mode 0600 rather than whatever the umask
    allowed, which was world-readable under the default 022. Captures
    routinely contain tokens and should not widen with -o or -d. They are
    written under a private name and renamed into place, so the image is
    never briefly visible at a readable mode and no half-written PNG
    appears under the final name. This is a behaviour change for anyone
    writing into a directory they then share.
  * Saving no longer follows a symlink sitting at the destination. Screenshot
    names are timestamps and so are guessable, which meant that when saving
    into a world-writable directory another user could pre-plant a link under
    the name about to be used and have the PNG written straight through it,
    over a file of their choosing that you own. The link is now replaced.
  * The Wayland capture path no longer leaves a copy of the raw screen in
    /tmp when GNOME Shell writes to a path other than the one it was given.
    The stray file was the unannotated screen, so it outlived any redaction
    drawn on the capture that was kept.

 -- Shubshub  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 16:50:00 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.17.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * Region screenshot tool bound to a hotkey, defaulting to Print. Freezes
    the screen, puts a toolbar on every monitor, and captures on demand.
  * Ten tools: region, freehand pen, highlighter, line/rectangle/circle/
    arrow, measure, redaction, pixelation, colour picker, numbered steps
    and text. Adding another is one file plus a registry line.
  * Undo and redo over everything, including setting the capture region.
  * Result goes to the clipboard and to ~/Pictures/Screenshots, with the
    path on standard output so it composes in scripts.
  * Shutter sound, and a notification offering to open the image or show it
    in its folder.
  * Hover tooltips on every toolbar button.
  * Installing the hotkey frees whatever GNOME binding holds the key and
    restores it on uninstall.

 -- Shubshub  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 15:20:00 +1200
